{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310574,"nonce":"kRKUcXVOKWIWOAFxW4461Sou8y8=","title":"FIXED de609a53 for user modules: a cross-module `new X(oneArg)` was SILENTLY replaced by that arg when X's module lowered after the caller. ...","message":"A `new X(oneArgument)` whose class lives in another module was silently replaced\nby that argument whenever X's module was MIR-lowered AFTER the constructing\nmodule. The constructor never ran; exit 0, wrong answer.\n\n**FIXED de609a53** for user modules. Conformance byte-identical: 431 PASS, ZERO\nstatus changes across all 1240 cases (the corpus is almost entirely\nsingle-module, so it never covered this — which is why a silent wrong answer in\n`Array.map` survived in shipped code).\n\n\nI first root-caused this to the `Xml` \u003c-\u003e `haxe.xml.Parser` cycle. **Wrong.**\n`CANONICAL_CYCLE_REPRO/acyclic/` is a plain DAG (`Main -\u003e Aaa -\u003e Mmm`, Mmm\nreferences nothing) and the constructor is still dropped. Order alone suffices.\n\n\nSame program, same compile order, only the argument count differs:\n\n```\nnew Mmm()        -\u003e CTOR0 / done      CORRECT\nnew Mmm(s)       -\u003e done              BUG      \u003c- constructor dropped\nnew Mmm(s, 7)    -\u003e CTOR2 a7 / done   CORRECT\n```\n\nA working forward-ref path ALREADY SHIPS: `construction.rs:822-854` emits\nalloc + a name-keyed stub via `cross_module_constructor_fqn_key`, and\n`fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` rebinds it. The `args.len() == 1` guard at\n`construction.rs:436` was the ONLY thing diverting the one-arg shape out of it.\n(An earlier claim that 0/2 args \"SIGILL with no diagnostic\" was FALSE — measured.)\n\n\n`unlowered_constructor_class` (`ir/mir/helpers/classes.rs`) asks the parsed\ndeclaration one question — is this a constructible user `class` declaring its\nOWN `new` of compatible arity — and on yes skips the wrap so the existing path\nruns. Safety properties that matter:\n\n- The key is derived from `cross_module_constructor_fqn_key`, **the same\n  function that names the stub**, so guard and stub cannot disagree. An\n  unmatched stub does not degrade quietly — it SIGILLs.\n- `StaticSigIndex` gains `ctor_params` (it explicitly discarded `new`), recorded\n  only from a `TypeDeclaration::Class` arm, so abstracts and inherit-only\n  subclasses answer no.\n- The import loader records the path it had already resolved and threw away, so\n  a declaring file is parsed only when some `new` needs it. **Compile order is\n  untouched.**\n- Allocation on the newly reachable path is floored by the old arg-count guess:\n  it can only ever allocate the same or more.\n- `RAYZOR_NO_XMODULE_CTOR=1` restores the old behaviour — attribute a regression\n  in ONE run instead of a rebuild-and-bisect.\n\nThe wrap still fires for everything else, and that is load-bearing: unresolved\nabstracts, `extern class` with a 1-arg `new`, generic classes, and every `new`\ninside haxe-std.\n\n\n`sig_index.rs`: `declared_constructor_arity` no longer tests the path for\nhaxe-std (extern/abstract/bodyless `new` already record no arity), generic\nclasses record an arity, and `known_file` accepts the loader's BARE key only when\nthe path's trailing components spell the package. `classes.rs`: the blanket\nhaxe-std early return is gone. g2d (`StringTools.iterator`) and g2c\n(`StringKeyValueIterator`) FIXED; battery 434 -\u003e 435, 0 lost, 0 moved.\n\n**Two residuals, DIFFERENT mechanisms (not the constructor):**\n- `htmlEscape` still \"\" (g2a/g2b): the ctor is now called, but `for (code in new\n  StringIteratorUnicode(s))` has NO LOOP in the MIR. `for_in.rs` class-iterator\n  branch: `match (get_function_id(hasNext), get_function_id(next)) { (Some,Some)\n  =\u003e .., _ =\u003e return }` — symbols pre-registered, function ids absent (module\n  lowers later) -\u003e the WHOLE LOOP WAS SILENTLY DROPPED (and when the SYMBOLS are\n  absent too, the fallback chain ends in a bare `return`). FIXED 0bca29a3: the static\n  sig index answers `declares_instance_method`; both entry points become\n  `\u003cfqn\u003e.hasNext`/`\u003cfqn\u003e.next` forward-ref stubs the fixup pass binds\n  (`unlowered_iterator_stubs` in classes.rs). g2a/g2b FIXED; battery 435 -\u003e 436\n  (Issue12700), 0 lost, 0 moved; stdlib harness OK.\n- The maps' `keyValueIterator()` (g4a/g4b, exit 132/134): the\n  `MapKeyValueIterator` ctor is now found (path=bare-name), then its body calls\n  `map.keys()` on `map: IMap\u003cK,V\u003e` -\u003e \"`IMap.keys` was never compiled\" — interface\n  dispatch onto an extern map resolves to the INTERFACE's bodyless declaration\n  because interface vtable slots are filled only for `function_map` methods, which\n  runtime-mapped externs lack. Separate defect (D12), not compile order.\n\n\nA 9-agent workflow (3 readers, 2 experimenters, 4 refuters; 0 refutations; repros\n`scratchpad/wf_userrepro/s2_user_wraps_stdlib_B`, `wf_extprobe/g2d`) established:\n\n1. **Compile-order inversion the toposort cannot see.** `[import-order]` is only\n   the first-pass ATTEMPT order. The constructed iterator classes\n   (`haxe.iterators.StringIterator`, `StringKeyValueIterator`,\n   `StringIteratorUnicode`, `MapKeyValueIterator`) sit in import 2-cycles with\n   their users (`StringTools`; the three maps). cb5ee997 schedules them first,\n   but their first-pass `try_compile_import` FAILS (`StringIterator.next` calls\n   `StringTools.unsafeCodeAt` -\u003e E0100 \"class not registered\", StringTools' type\n   is `TypeId(u32::MAX)`), they go to the retry queue (compilation.rs:3941-3984)\n   and lower AFTER StringTools/the maps, which lowered against an\n   `import_constructor_name_map` snapshot without their key. All six\n   `has_constructor` lookups in `lower_new` miss. Actual dump-header order proves\n   it (StringTools.hx before StringIterator.hx). Errors of the failed first pass\n   are debug!-only and truncated — no env probe shows them.\n2. **Both haxe-std gates refuse the de609a53 recovery**: `classes.rs`\n   `unlowered_constructor_class` (`source_file.contains(\"haxe-std\")`) AND\n   `sig_index.rs declared_constructor_arity` (`is_user_declared` = path not in\n   haxe-std; plus `record_ctor` refusing generics). That is why lifting ONE was\n   inert. The user-module twin of the SAME cycle (v8-v11) passes: alloc + named\n   `\u003cfqn\u003e.new` stub, rebound by `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs`.\n3. **Key-shape trap**: the loader records `StringIterator` BARE (String.hx\n   references it bare) while the stub key is `haxe.iterators.StringIterator.new`;\n   `known_files.get(fqn)` misses. Resolve via the bare name ONLY when the path's\n   trailing components spell the package (`haxe/iterators/StringIterator.hx`).\n\nThe \"NOT value-wrap failures / SYMBOL-PUN mismatch\" attribution below predates\nthe compile-order commits and is wrong for these sites: `RAYZOR_CTOR_DEBUG=1`\nprints `[CTOR] class=haxe.iterators.StringIterator path=VALUE-WRAP` and\n`fn @StringTools.iterator ... { ret $0 }`. The SYMBOL-PUN line for\n`StringIteratorUnicode` is a DIFFERENT, correct `new` (in its own module).\n\n\n1. **Any `new` lowered inside haxe-std.** `[\"a\",\"b\"].map(x-\u003ex+\"!\")` -\u003e\n   `[\u003cunknown type ...\u003e!, ...]`; `StringTools.htmlEscape` -\u003e garbage bytes;\n   `urlEncode` -\u003e empty. All exit 0.\n   **These are NOT value-wrap failures** — widening the recovery to stdlib fixes\n   NOTHING (measured, see below). Per the exploration: `htmlEscape`/`urlEncode`\n   fail through a SYMBOL-PUN path with mismatched TypeIds\n   (`class_type=TypeId(451) resolved_type=TypeId(1551)`, a module-local\n   IrFunctionId used as a cross-module key — [[bugs-irfunctionid-is-module-local]]);\n   the Map family are bodyless `#if eval` externs (`haxe/ds/StringMap.hx:77-83`).\n2. **A true cycle whose modules are in DIFFERENT packages** — fails at EVERY\n   arity (0/2-arg crash too), so the stub cannot bind at all. Separate defect.\n   `scratchpad/k2`, `k4`, `c1` are all this shape and are byte-identical with\n   the fix on and off. Same-package cycles ARE fixed (`CANONICAL_CYCLE_REPRO/repro`).\n\n\nLifting the haxe-std exclusion in `unlowered_constructor_class` is **inert**.\nThere are TWO independent stdlib gates: the lowering-side one in\n`helpers/classes.rs:780`, and a DECLARING-side one at `tast/sig_index.rs:162`\n(`!path.contains(\"haxe-std\")`). With only the first lifted, `Array.iterator` is\nstill `ret $0` and `Array.map`/`htmlEscape`/`urlEncode` are unchanged. Verified\nby A/B on one binary behind a temporary flag, then reverted rather than shipping\na dead gate.\n\n\n`scratchpad/CANONICAL_CYCLE_REPRO/` — `run.sh` runs the oracle, runs rayzor,\ndoes 10 cold reps, exits non-zero unless it reproduces. Arms: `repro`\n(silent wrong answer), `control`, `acyclic`, `segv`. `repro` and `control`\ndiffer by **one never-called function**.\n\n- **Observe a CONSTRUCTOR SIDE EFFECT** (a counter in a THIRD module), never a\n  returned field. The old `1701667150` is an out-of-bounds heap read: it tracks\n  the input string, changes run to run, is 0 under `--llvm`, and becomes a\n  SIGSEGV if the field is renamed. The counter must NOT live in the entry module\n  — that creates a back-edge and turns the control into a second repro.\n- **The BLADE cache is a hidden input.** Of the 8 states of (stdlib blades, user\n  blades, entry MIR), 7 show the bug and exactly one hides it — and that state\n  makes the control throw. `rm -rf .rayzor` before every rep; never A/B this\n  without controlling cache state.\n- Probes: `RAYZOR_CTOR_DEBUG=1` (a `path=VALUE-WRAP` line now names the wrap\n  explicitly; absence of any `[CTOR] class=X` line also means it fired),\n  `RAYZOR_PROFILE_TYPECHECK_FILES=1` (compile order).\n- The Bash tool's shell is **zsh**, which does NOT word-split unquoted\n  variables — run sweeps from an explicit `bash` script file.\n\nRelated: [[bugs-xml-parse-three-remaining-blockers]],\n[[bugs-irfunctionid-is-module-local]], [[bugs-assignment-rereads-its-target]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cross_module_new_dropped_by_compile_order` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}